1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,767
What changes when AI starts doing the research that builds the next generation of AI?

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:10,100
The important shift is a feedback loop: better systems can

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:14,433
help produce better successors, which can then accelerate the work again.

4
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:21,133
The evidence already shows substantial automation, but the numbers need care.

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,467
The paper cites Anthropic reporting that AI wrote more than

6
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:28,733
eighty percent of approved code by May twenty twenty-six.

7
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:32,533
Separately, twenty-six percent of research and development work

8
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:37,367
was completed autonomously, with high-level human supervision, by August.

9
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:41,467
Writing code and owning a research workflow are different measures.

10
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:46,900
A research cycle needs an idea, an implementation,

11
00:00:47,267 --> 00:00:50,133
an experiment, and a judgment about the result.

12
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,967
Making the implementation faster helps.

13
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:58,233
But an idea still has to survive testing, and somebody

14
00:00:58,233 --> 00:01:01,267
has to decide whether the apparent improvement is real.

15
00:01:02,967 --> 00:01:03,967
Now close the loop.

16
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:07,600
A stronger model helps run that research cycle.

17
00:01:07,967 --> 00:01:11,100
If the cycle produces a more capable or more

18
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:15,133
efficient successor, that successor can join the research team.

19
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,733
The team may then do more useful work on the same hardware.

20
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:23,933
That is the mechanism behind a possible software-driven intelligence explosion.

21
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:31,467
The key question is whether expanding research capacity can overcome diminishing returns.

22
00:01:31,833 --> 00:01:36,800
The paper calls this balance returns to research effort, represented by r.

23
00:01:37,133 --> 00:01:43,533
In its simplified model, values above one support acceleration while that condition holds.

24
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:47,467
This is an assumption to investigate, not a universal law.

25
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:53,133
One calculation suggests a tenfold increase in the pace of

26
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:57,300
progress within about one and a half years after full automation.

27
00:01:57,633 --> 00:02:00,833
That result assumes research returns stay at the

28
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:04,133
estimated levels and no other bottlenecks emerge.

29
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:09,067
It is not a prediction that a tenfold speedup will arrive eighteen months from today.

30
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:16,833
There are four broad sources of friction: diminishing returns, limits on experimental compute and

31
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:22,133
data, tasks that remain hard to automate, and processes that simply take time.

32
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:27,233
A thousand proposed experiments are not a thousand validated discoveries.

33
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:33,100
More agents cannot, by themselves, make a long training run finish instantly.

34
00:02:34,833 --> 00:02:39,667
Faster research could bring useful scientific and medical advances closer.

35
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,767
But the same acceleration can leave less time

36
00:02:42,767 --> 00:02:47,900
to evaluate systems, preserve human control, and adapt institutions.

37
00:02:48,267 --> 00:02:53,533
The paper also warns that concentrating research capability could weaken checks on power.

38
00:02:55,233 --> 00:03:00,667
Consider a research loop with an explicit checkpoint before each successor is deployed.

39
00:03:01,033 --> 00:03:04,167
The checkpoint asks what improved, what failed,

40
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,467
and whether the evidence justifies expanding access.

41
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,733
If generating the next model becomes fast, these

42
00:03:10,733 --> 00:03:14,667
checks become more consequential, not optional overhead.

43
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:22,133
The authors propose three priorities: clearer reporting on research automation, ways

44
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:26,900
to steer or pause risky development, and preparation for wider impacts.

45
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:32,900
The practical takeaway is to track the whole loop: useful research output, the bottlenecks

46
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:37,467
that remain, and whether people can still understand and control what happens next.
